## INTRODUCTION
It is of great importance to discriminate the subtle difference among molecules with similar physicochemical properties in diverse areas, such as medical diagnostics,1 environmental monitoring,2 toxicological analysis,3 or gas separation.4 By present, diverse biological/chemical sensors have been well developed,5, 6 but most of them required a single host or a number of hosts for the selective detection of a single target (Figure 1a). Recently, molecular decoding strategy has attracted intensive attention for multiple-target differentiation,7, 8 which rendered a single host domain to accommodate diverse analytes, with each distinguished from the others by transducing individual specific host-guest interaction into corresponding output signal (Figure 1b). It should be noted that molecular decoding strategy mostly required innovative transduction mechanism involving the intrinsic nature of guest analytes. However, to date, it is still challenging to integrate a decent transduction technique to the development of an effective decoder for multiple-target recognition and differentiation .
By present, among the transduction techniques involved in chemical sensors, luminescent methods have become simple and sensitive approaches which could enable on-site analysis avoiding complicated instrumentation,9-11 with the luminescence easily observed as the effective visual output. Furthermore, compared to most single-color luminescence, multiplecolor luminescence could render better accuracy in a ratiometric way which is more convenient and selective for molecular recognition.12 Based on that, ideal molecular decoders composed of multiple emitters in diverse emission ranges could be designed and fabricated, which would respond to diverse molecules in different manners and realize specific recognition for a particular target in an effective way.13 However, it should be noted that this strategy is mostly hindered by the tailored synthesis of objective probes and poor decoding capability up to now,14-16 which still needs to be greatly explored for further development.
By present, lanthanide (Ln) metal-organic frameworks (Ln-MOFs), constructed with Ln ions and organic bridging ligands, has become a promising subfamily of MOFs for chemical sensing due to their attractive photoluminescence (PL) properties.17-19 In principle, based on the “antenna effect”,20 Ln-MOFs can absorb photonic energy via light irradiation by the organic ligands, which will subsequently transfer the energy from the triplet state of ligand to Ln ions, resulting in the strong Ln-based emission with sharp bands, large Stokes shift, bright emission in the visible regions.21 The essence to this internal energy transfer in MOFs lies in the use of organic ligand with suitable triplet energy state $(\mathrm{T}_{1}),^{22-25}$ and mixed-Ln with multiple PL emissions can be simply integrated into the MOFs by mixing the Ln ions and organic ligand as the synthetic precursors.26-28 Compared to other multiple-emission systems,13 mixed-Ln MOFs as the decoders possess the merits including easy design, facile fabrication and easily detectable color change by naked eyes. Moreover, the inherent confinement effect via the nanosized pores is conducive to effective host-guest interaction, which is essential to molecular decoding. Therefore, the mixedLn MOFs have been developed for multi-target detection, molecular decoding, and anti-counterfeiting.29-32 However, most mixed-Ln MOFs only focused on Ln-based luminescence,33, 34 while little attention was paid to the organic ligands.24, 25 In this respect, taking full advantage of the luminescence derived from the organic moiety  in the mixed-Ln MOFs would lead to a new approach for molecular decoding.
Therefore, we herein chose 1, 3, 5-benzenetrisbenzoic acid $\mathrm{(H_{3}B T B)}$ with a triplet excited energy state of $23426\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ as the ligand to construct a Tb/Eu-MOF,35 which could integrate the red $(\mathrm{Eu}^{3+})$ , green $(\mathrm{Tb}^{3+})$ , and blue emissions (BTB) in a single host. This $\mathrm{Tb/Eu(BTB)}$ could serve as an ideal candidate for molecular decoding by involving a wide coverage in the visible region of luminescence. The ultra-high decoding capability of the $\mathrm{Tb/Eu(BTB)}$ could be realized through fine-tuned modulation of diverse energy transfer pathways in the $\mathrm{Tb/Eu(BTB)}$ , based on orthogonally mannered host-guest interactions. The prepared $\mathrm{Tb/Eu(BTB)}$ was tested for the recognition of a broad range of molecules, including homologues, isomers, enantiomers, and even deuterated isotopomers. It turned out that the subtle difference among these molecules could be distinguished, based on specific PL variation, revealing the unique mode of host-guest interaction between the Tb/Eu(BTB) and the target with specific polarity, size, shape or configuration. Moreover, the decoder showed satisfactory pH stability and self-reference capability to guarantee the practical utility in real-world applications to some extent. Based on these properties, the developed sensor could further achieve on-site analysis.
## EXPERIMENTAL SECTION
Materials. All chemicals used in this work were of analytical grade or better. Formamide, N, N-dimethylformamide (DMF), methanol, ethanol, and other alcohols were purchased from Kelong Chemical Reagent Co. Ltd. (Chengdu, China). 1, 3, 5-Benzenetrisbenzoic acid $\mathrm{(H_{3}B T B)}$ was purchased from Chemsoon Co., Ltd. (Shanghai, China). Terbium(III) nitrate hexahydrate $(\mathrm{Tb}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{3}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})$ and europium(III) nitrate hexahydrate $(\mathrm{Eu}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{3}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})$ were purchased from Aladdin (Shanghai, China). N, N-diethylformamide (DEF), N, N-dimethylacetamide (DMAC), N, N-diethylacetamide (DEAC), R/SAlaninol, R/S-Valinol, R/S-Phenylethylamine, R/S-2-butanol, and other solvents were purchased from Energy Chemical (Shanghai, China). Ultra-pure water (resistance $>18.2\ \mathrm{M}\Omega\cdot\mathrm{cm}$ was produced with a water purification system (PCUJ-10, Chengdu Pure Technology Co., Ltd., Chengdu, China). All chemicals here were used without further purification.
Synthesis of $\mathbf{Ln}(\mathbf{BTB})(\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{O})$ . The $\mathrm{Ln}(\mathbf{B}\mathrm{TB})(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathbf{O})$ $\mathrm{{Ln}=}$ Eu or Tb) studied here was obtained according to a previously reported method with a slightly modified procedure.36 Specifically, for $\mathrm{Tb}(\mathbf{B}\mathrm{TB})(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathbf{O})$ (marked as Ln-MOF-1), a mixture of $\mathrm{Tb}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{3}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ ( $\mathrm{68~mg}$ , 0.15 mmol) and $\mathrm{\bfH}_{3}\mathrm{\bfBTB}$ ( $66~\mathrm{mg}$ , 0.15 mmol) were dissolved in $30~\mathrm{mL}$ DMF. The obtained solution was heated in the microwave reactor under autogenous pressure at $120^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , $500~\mathrm{W}$ for $^{\textrm{1h}}$ , and then cooled down to room temperature. The product of colorless crystals  obtained by centrifugation, and then washed for three times with DMF and ethanol. The obtained solid product was vacuum-dried at $100^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for 12 h to obtain the final product. The $\mathrm{Eu(BTB)(H_{2}O)}$ (Ln-MOF-6) was obtained by a similar procedure as described for Ln-MOF1 except that $\mathrm{Eu(NO_{3})_{3}}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ was used instead of $\mathrm{Tb}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{3}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ . The mixed Ln-MOFs 2-5 were synthesized similarly to Ln-MOF-1 except for the use of a mixture of $\mathrm{Tb}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{3}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ and $\mathrm{Eu(NO_{3})_{3}}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ with molar ratios of 99.5:0.5, 98:2, 95:5, and 90:10, respectively.
Chemical sensing procedures. The desolvated materials were used for each spectrophotometric measurement. For chemical sensing, the desolvated Ln-MOF-2 particles were dispersed in ethanol to prepare a stock suspension with a concentration of $2~\mathrm{mg/mL}$ for later use. For analysis based on colorimetric sensing in $4\mathrm{mL}$ glass bottles, Ln-MOF-2 $2\mathrm{mg/mL}$ , 200 $\upmu\mathrm{L})$ was added, and then thoroughly vacuum-dried at $100^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $16\mathrm{~h~}$ . Subsequently, $2~\mathrm{mL}$ analyte solution was added to the glass bottles pre-modified by Ln-MOF-2, with the MOFs particles homogeneously dispersed in the solution to obtain a fine slurry. The slurry was ultimately transferred into a quartz cuvette and then put it under a light source set at a wavelength of $302{\mathrm{nm}}$ for visual observation, with the according photos taken with the digital camera. Photoluminescence spectra were recorded with a Hitachi F-7000 fluorescence spectrometer (Hitachi, Japan) using a $350\mathrm{nm}$ optical filter. The CIE1931 chromaticity coordinates were calculated from the obtained emission spectra by using the ColorCalculator software developed by Osram Sylvania, Inc. Besides, luminescence lifetime measurements were performed in liquid for some selected samples.
Sample preparation for mechanism study. For sensing mechanism studies, the desolvated Ln-MOF-2 particles were immersed in different solvents for $16\mathrm{~h~}$ , then vacuum-dried at room temperature for $16\mathrm{h}$ . For analinol, due to its high boiling point, the drying temperature was set at $60~^{\mathrm{{o}}}\mathrm{{C}}$ . The obtained materials were used for each XRD, Raman, FT-IR, quantum yields, and luminescence measurement.
## RESULTS AND DISCUSSION
The colorless crystals of the $\mathrm{Ln}(\mathrm{BTB})(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})$ $\mathrm{Ln}=\mathrm{Eu}$ or Tb) were synthesized via the simple reaction between $\mathrm{{H_{3}B T B}}$ and nitrate of $\mathrm{Ln}^{3+}$ in DMF at $120^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{\textrm{1h}}$ under microwave irradiation, followed by thorough rinsing with DMF and ethanol and overnight activation at $100^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . By varying the molar ratio of $\mathrm{Eu}^{3+}/\mathrm{Tb}^{3+}$ , four bimetallic Ln-MOFs were obtained (see details in the Experimental Section). Based on the powder Xray diffraction pattern (Figure 2a), each had the isostructual to the two parent Ln-MOFs (with only Tb or Eu), and all the six Ln-MOFs crystallizes in a homochiral space group, the bulk materials exhibit a racemic mixture (Figure S1 & S2).36 Moreover, these Ln-MOFs show good thermal-stability (Figure S3).
As depicted in the Jablonski diagram (Figure S10), there should be diverse pathways for the energy transfer within the framework of the $\mathrm{Eu/Tb}$ bimetallic Ln-MOFs.37 In short, the absorbed photo energy by ligand first induced the BTB emission in the form of fluorescence and phosphorescence which will be greatly suppressed if the energy transfer from BTB to Eu and/or Tb occurs, with the Eu accepted the energy from both BTB and Tb in priority due to the lowest energy level of ${}^{5}\mathrm{D}_{0}$ . The energy transfer pathways within the Ln-MOFs should be very sensitive to the host-guest interaction with specific target. In general, if the $\mathrm{Eu/Tb}$ emission was quenched or the BTB- $.{\mathrm{Eu/Tb}}$ energy transfer was suppressed, the BTB emission would dominate the PL of the Ln-MOFs locating in the blue range in the chromaticity diagram (CD). If the BTB emission was enforced without any interference to the energy transfer from BTB to $\mathrm{Eu/Tb}$ , the $\mathrm{Eu/Tb}$ emission would be facilitated, with the Tb emission dominating the PL if the Eu emission was suppressed or the Tb-Eu energy transfer was inhibited. In the CD, the PL of the LnMOFs would move towards the red range with the $\mathrm{Eu/Tb}$ ratio increased but the green range with the ratio decreased. Since the preferred energy transfer from BTB or Tb to Eu, the $\mathrm{Eu/Tb}$ ratio in the Ln-MOFs should be cautiously controlled and optimized, or else the Tb emission would be severely suppressed. Therefore, the Ln-MOF-2 ( $0.5\%$ Eu and $99.5\%$ Tb) was chosen as the probe for the molecular docoding.
The aforementioned host-guest interaction caused by the target molecule can be realized either on the Eu/Tb metallic site or the BTB ligand. In principle, the Eu or Tb emission would be weakened by the target species through colliding, which is approximately in extent inversely varied with the sixth power of the distance (r) between Ln and HX oscillators (e.g. OH or NH groups).38 Therefore, the oscillator strength and the distance between the Ln-MOF-2 and the target molecules could play an important role to affect the PL of the Ln ions. Besides, the singlet and triplet excited energy state of BTB could also be modulated by the target molecules with different polarity and pH, which could further affect the PL of the BTB and the Ln ions.39, 40 For initial evaluation and demonstration, eleven different molecules were mixed one by one with the Ln-MOF-2, with the specific PL observed by naked eyes after $5\ \mathrm{min}$ for each molecule (Figure 3). Therefore, the great potential in the LnMOF-2 for the decoding of a broad spectrum of molecules from different subfamilies should be highly expected.
It was initially revealed in Figure 3 that the polarity, functionality, molecular size, shape, and configuration could behave as the primary factors influencing the host-guest interaction. For further investigation and exploration of the Ln-MOF-2 for molecular decoding, the homologous molecules including carboxylic acids, alcohols, amines, ethers, amides, ketones, halogenated methanes, mono-substituted benzenes and deuterated isotopomers were chosen as the representative targets (Figure S11-18).
The polarity and potential $\pi-\pi$ stacking interactions were first studied. As revealed in detail (Figure 4a & b), the molecules easily deprotonated (e.g. carboxylic acid) or with higher polarity (e.g. diamine) could coordinate to $\mathrm{Eu/Tb}$ and the crystalline structure of the Ln-MOF-2 would change or even collapse (Figure S21), so the Ln-MOF-2 showed ligand (BTB)- dependent luminescence, locating in the blue range of CD. The polar molecules (e.g. amine or alcohol) with moderate coordination capability could cause changes of the coordination environment of Ln ions and further affect their emissions.41 Moreover, the non-polar molecules such as cyclohexane or carbontetrachloride could hardly interact with Eu/Tb or BTB, with the PL emission of the Ln-MOF-2 almost unchanged. The $\pi\mathfrak{-}\pi$ stacking could suppress the BTB emission but promote the energy transfer from BTB to $\mathrm{Eu/Tb}$ , so the weaker emission for BTB but the stronger emission for Tb was induced by benzene rather than cyclohexane (Figure 4c & d).
The size or shape of the targets could also affect their effective access to the Ln-MOF-2 and further interaction due to steric hindrance to different extents. In terms of size, for instance, among the homologous straight-chain alcohols, nbutanol could cause the most obvious change in the PL of LnMOF-2 most probably by its suitable size to be accommodated in the MOF pore (Figure 5a & b). With the increase in the length of the substituted alkyl group to benzene, the color moved gradually from the green toward the blue range, illustrating that the increasing size of targets could lead to more steric hindrance for accessing BTB and less $\pi-\pi$ stacking interaction (Figure 5c & d). In terms of shape, distinguishing among isomers is always an urgent task for molecular decoding. Taking butanols and xylenes as the exemplars, it is exciting to find that both can be unambiguously differentiated among each group of the isomers. In particular, for the butanols, n-butanol could coordinate to the metallic sites most effectively, causing the obvious red shift of BTB emission, while for the t-butanol with the most significant steric hindrance, it is difficult to access the Ln-MOF-2 to influence the PL (Figure 6a & b). Similarly, for xylenes, the subtle difference in the molecular shapes among them could  affect their accessing and further interaction with BTB via - stackings at different levels, leading to obviously different PL emissions of the Ln-MOF-2 (Figure 6c & d).
The configuration of the targets, especially enantiomers, with almost the same chemical or physical properties between each other except the capability to rotate plane-polarized light, could also be distinguished by the Ln-MOF-2 which could be endowed with more practical utility because fast and facile chiral recognition is one of the most challenging tasks for real analysis, especially by naked eyes.42 In general, the homochirally structured sensors are preferred over achiral ones or racemic mixtures for chiral recognition, which have rarely been developed, however.43 Fortunately, the Ln-MOF-2 here could become an ideal alternative for chiral recognition to the present homochiral probes.
As shown in Figure 7, four pairs of enantiomers could be distinguished, respectively, by naked eyes.  For each pair, the S configuration could lead to stronger Eu/Tb emission than the R configuration. Furthermore, the Ln-MOF-2 was used to semiquantify the $\mathrm{ee\%}$ values $(-100\%$ , 0, $20\%$ , $40\%$ , $60\%$ , $80\%$ , and $100\%$ ) of the enantiomers (e.g. alaninol and phenethylamine) (Figure S19&S20). The PL of the Ln-MOF-2 moved gradually from the blue range toward the green range in CD as the $\mathrm{e.e.\%}$ (i.e. the content of S-configuration) increased, and the LnMOF-2 could provide a particular PL readout from the enantiomers with a specific $\mathrm{e.e.\%}$ value distinguishable from the others. This illustrated that the Ln-MOF-2 could be employed as an efficient sensor for chiral analysis in both qualitative and quantitative ways, mostly ascribed to the homochiral structure of the Ln-MOF-2 with high shape selectivity.
Furthermore, it is exciting to find out that as a decoder, LnMOF-2 could even distinguish deuterated isotopomers based on visual output. As shown in Figure 8a & 8b, Ln-MOF-2 can emit green and purple luminescence in chloroform and deuterochloroform, respectively. The red-shifted as well as enforced PL of BTB and weakened PL of Ln ions may be ascribed to the change in vibration energy and dipole moment of C-H in chloroform caused by deuterium isotope effects.31, 44-46 However, the Ln-MOF-2 exhibited different PL only in intensity in $_\mathrm{H_{2}O}$ and $\mathrm{D}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ (Figure 8c & 8d), mostly due to slightly different quenching capability of OH and OD on $\mathrm{Ln}^{3+}$ excited-state (particularly for $\mathrm{Eu}^{3+}$ ).31 This exciting discovery extended the decoding capability of the Ln-MOF-2 from molecule to the deuterated isotopomer level, further exploring its potential for a broad spectrum of decoding.
In order to further investigate the unique interaction between the Ln-MOF-2 and specific molecules, PXRD and Raman spectrometry were employed to find the differentially varied crystallinity and vibration mode of the Ln-MOF-2 caused by the target. Based on PXRD patterns, it could be seen that molecules with high coordination capability (e.g. acetic acid and ethylenediamine) could destroy the crystalline structure of the Ln-MOF-2 (Figure S21) and further eliminate the antenna effect, with the primary PXRD peaks all disappeared and only the emission of BTB remaining. The similar phenomenon was observed in R-alanniol, which could be attributed to the chiral selectivity of the Ln-MOF-2 with homochiral space (Figure S1, S22 & S28, Table S5). Isomers (e.g. butanol) could make the PXRD peaks (pink area in Figure 9a) of the Ln-MOF-2 split evidently mostly ascribed to the heterogeneous-phase intrabinding via coordination between Eu/Tb and hydroxyl groups.47 Notably, for t-butanol, due to the most significant steric hindrance, the lattice expansion in the Ln-MOF-2 should be more evident than that caused by the other isomers, leading to the most obvious peak shifting in PXRD pattern predicted by the Bragg’s equation.
Based on Raman spectra, taking butanol isomers for example, almost no changes to the Ln-MOF-2 were observed by that with distinct steric hindrance and lack of interaction (e.g. t-butanol), while the others induced the split bands around $1161\mathrm{cm}^{\cdot}$ 1 merged and the band at $1450\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ obviously widened, mostly due to the effective binding of hydroxyl groups to $\mathrm{Eu/Tb}$ , which further affected the systematic vibration of Ln-BTB coordination bonds. In particular, the merged bands around $1161~\mathrm{{cm}^{-1}}$ was the sharpest caused by n-butanol, illustrating the most effective coordination to Eu/Tb. Additional detailed information such as the assignment of Raman bands was provided in the SI (Figure S23-25 and Table S2).
Moreover, the binding of target molecules to $\mathrm{Eu/Tb}$ could change the coordination symmetry of the Ln-MOFs and modulate the energy of singlet or triplet excited state of BTB. This could be conducive to the energy transfer from BTB to $\mathrm{Eu/Tb}$ , which was verified by the change of PL lifetime, quantum yields. For example, the coordination of n-butanol to Ln-MOF2 was more effective than that of other isomers of butanol (e.g. t-butanol), which could lead to longer PL lifetime and higher quantum yield of $\mathrm{Tb}^{3+}$ in the Ln-MOF-2 (Table S3 & S7, Figure S26). However, the PL lifetime of $\mathrm{Tb}^{3+}$ in the Ln-MOF-2 kept almost unchanged after immersed in either $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ or ${\bf D}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ (Table S4, Figure S27) mostly due to the maintained coordination symmetry.
In addition, $\pi\cdot\pi$ stacking between BTB and guest molecule was also confirmed by Raman spectra, PL lifetime and according quantum yields (Figure 9d, S29,  and Table S6-S7). Although this interaction made almost no changes to the topological structure of the Ln-MOF-2 (Figure 9b), it would, however, restrict the free rotation of BTB and further improve the efficiency of inter-system crossing (ISC) and Ln emission, with the prolonged PL lifetime and increased quantum yields accordingly obtained (Table S3 & S5). Moreover, the change of quantum yields of the Ln-MOF-2 was minimal if mixed with nonpolar molecules (e.g. cyclohexane and carbon tetrachloride), illustrating the weakest interaction between them (Table S7).
It should also be mentioned that for PL-based analysis, especially in aqueous phase, such as medical diagnostics and environmental monitoring, pH value is an important factor. As shown in Figure S30, the Ln-MOF-2 immunes to the varied $\mathrm{pH}$ values from 5 to 10, with the PL kept almost the same, which should be applicable to most real analysis based on PL readouts of probes in aqueous phase. With the pH value varied from 6 to 2, it could be seen that the emission intensity of $\mathrm{Tb}^{3+}$ or ${\mathrm{Eu}}^{3+}$ increased first and then decreased, most probably due to the protonation lowering the energy of the singlet excited state and triplet excited state40. For the $\mathrm{\pH}$ value representing high acidity or basicity, only the emission of BTB was observed due to the collapse of the crystalline structure of the Ln-MOF-2 (Figure S30a) and further elimination of the aforementioned “antenna effect”. Moreover, as shown in Figure S31, the Ln-MOF-2 probe could show good self-calibration capability, with the emission intensity ratio $(\mathrm{I}_{545\ \mathrm{nm}}/\mathrm{I}_{615\ \mathrm{nm}})$ not obviously changed with the applied amount of the probes varied within certain concentration range. This attractive feature could further endow the Ln-MOF-2 with credible readout for real analysis, which was rarely reported in literature with previous probes for molecular decoding.
Let’s further compare Ln-MOF-2 with other previous PLbased molecular decoders (Table S8), in terms of single-wavelength excitation, full color readout, and visual decoding capability.  It turned out that the Ln-MOF-2 was obviously advantageous over the previously reported ones, and it could definitely be regarded as a powerful probe for a broad spectrum of molecular decoding.
## CONCLUSION
In summary, we developed a new molecular decoding strategy using a single bimetallic based Ln-MOFs probe for the differentiation of a broad spectrum of molecules. In a simple one-pot synthesis procedure with ligand (BTB) and mixed metal ions $\mathrm{Tb}^{3+}$ and ${\mathrm{Eu}}^{3+}$ ), a series of bimetallic Ln-MOFs can be prepared possessing distinctive red, green, and blue emissions when excited at a single wavelength. The obtained bimetallic Ln-MOFs showed excellent capability to distinguish the subtle difference among homologues, isomers, enantiomers, or even deuterated isotopomers, the by-present widest range of applicable analytes for decoding. The polarity, coordination capability, size and shape, configuration, or even deuterium substitution of molecules could more or less affect the host-guest interaction with the host Ln-MOFs, which could finely modulate the energy transfer pathways in the Ln-MOFs to generate a unique PL readout for specific target. The present work could further provide insights and strategies in the design of new multiple-luminescence materials for future molecular decoding and other applications such as ratiometric sensors, LED lighting, anti-counterfeiting, and electric nose/tongue fabrication. However, the situation can be complicated when it is used for diverse mixtures of real samples, and further in-depth study for various applications is definitely needed.
